6 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243696 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243696 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243696 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243696 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "88598f20-c925-534c-af55-d6bf9e3c545c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243724 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9923f324-3fc1-5d64-bb7b-b26016552565" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243944 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e309174-90b1-5dec-b490-77c152128db8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243944 #edb/ref 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244087 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244087 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e33547cc-9eda-5fa2-ada1-bbe7653d7d33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244087 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =10 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244089 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e19c29ed-ca08-5b08-881a-c2cea1937130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244089 #edb/ref 1074 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.730\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244092 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the expression $2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5b0b758-0715-5d36-8c00-3c3db2730695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244092 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244107 #edb/ref 1071 "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e548fde0-64fe-56ce-a880-74d36f5eace9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244107 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] & =7[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5c+11 \\\\ 13c-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7c+7 \\\\ 7c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5c+11=7c+7 \\\\ 13c-12=7c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c+11=7c+7\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n13c-12=7c\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
